ur 2s and four 7s) and I have additional outs for two pair or trips, the pot odds are extremely unfavorable. Since the pot was small before the shove, I am essentially being asked to call an amount equal to the potential winnings (1:1 odds), requiring roughly 50% equity to break even.\n\nAgainst a range that includes sets, two pair, overpairs, or made straights, my equity is likely around 35-40%. Additionally, since I am currently leading the cumulative match, taking a high-variance, negative expected value (EV) gamble is strategically unsound. Preserving the lead by avoiding marginal all-in calls is the optimal strategy here.\n\nReason:\nThe opponent's massive overbet destroys the pot odds required to chase a straight draw or call with middle pair. I need approximately 50% equity to call profitably, but likely have far less against a strong shoving range. Furthermore, protecting the cumulative chip lead takes priority over high-risk plays.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
